WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.820
The entire kind of arc of human history

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:04.700
has really been defined

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.950
by how we harness and capture energy.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.020
You know, initially starting

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.830
with us leveraging things like fire

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.930
and then later coal and
petroleum fuel sources

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.310
for our energy needs.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.990
And really the great opportunity
going forward is this idea

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.890
that we can capture
renewable energy sources,

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:22.660
store that energy in batteries

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.880
and then use that to power our lives.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.360
Advances in battery design
over the past few decades

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.570
have made modern technology
possible, but it's not enough.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.090
We need better, cheaper,
more energy dense batteries

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.640
if we're going to make
electric cars ubiquitous

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.703
and save the planet.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.440
Now companies are on the
verge of battery breakthroughs

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.943
that could change the world.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:46.693
The battery industry

00:00:46.693 --> 00:00:49.520
has been kind of stuck
making batteries one way

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.320
for like 50 years and we think

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.043
it's just time for that to change.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.400
A battery looks like a black
box quite literally sometimes

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.813
but inside it is a very
complex mixture of chemicals.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.010
There are four components.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.490
There are two electrodes,
cathode and anode,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.830
there's a separator

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.410
and then there's a liquid
electrolyte typically.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.320
And the electrolyte's job is to be able

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.250
to shuttle irons between
the two electrodes.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.530
And that's what charges the battery

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.240
and that's what allows the
battery to be then discharged

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.543
and produce power.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.730
Among the everyday technologies,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.170
batteries are perhaps one of the oldest

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.000
because batteries were invented even

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.880
before electricity was invented

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.460
which is to say there was no
way to generate electricity

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:54.460
until somebody made a battery
and that was back in 1799

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.730
by an Italian scientist named Volta.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:02.730
And what he created was
actually called a voltaic pile.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.830
It wasn't even called a battery back then.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.210
And it was called a pile because

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.480
it was literally a pile of
two different types of metal

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.270
in this case, copper and zinc separated

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.350
by typically a piece of cardboard
that was dipped in vinegar

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.950
To get a feel for how a battery works,

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.730
we decided to build our own.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.970
I got a copper sheet, I
got a couple zinc sheets.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.160
I cut them into one by one squares

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.170
and I also got a coffee filter

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.053
that I cut into one inch squares too.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:40.070
Right, just to start off,
keep a simple aluminum foil

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:41.443
at the bottom.

00:02:41.443 --> 00:02:42.580
Okay.
So that you're able to test

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.490
whether the battery is working or not.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.260
On top of you put a copper sheet.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.990
And so now you have to
dip the coffee filter

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.440
into a solution of salt and water.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:52.800
Okay.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.573
Then you put your coffee filter.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.650
Done.
Then you place

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.003
the zinc sheet on top.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.100
You also have a voltmeter which basically

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.460
will be able to measure the voltage

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.760
that this battery will have.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.240
Okay so we're getting 0.74 volts.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.800
Yeah, that sounds about right.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.120
Yeah.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.870
Now you can connect a
copper cable on either ends

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.490
and you could solider that on

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.800
then you can connect
it to say an LED light

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.850
and that should light up.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:29.220
The more cells you pile
on the higher the voltage.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.630
We piled on 10 layers of zinc and copper

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.023
to see if we could power up an LED light.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.860
So let's test it with an LED light.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:42.560
Okay, it's the moment of truth here.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.393
There we go.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.853
And that's electricity.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.159
Cool.

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:52.660
Batteries have come a long
way since the voltaic pile

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:55.860
but they're still made up of
the full basic components,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.800
anode, cathode, separator,
and electrolyte.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.260
The current state of the
art lithium-ion battery

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.900
is small, light and relatively powerful

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.960
making everything from mobile devices

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.603
to electric cars possible.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:12.570
But in order to make
de-carbonization a reality,

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.023
batteries need to get much better.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.510
We are quite far away from the limits

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.230
of what a battery can do.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.040
Among the different things about batteries

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.310
that still need to be improved

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.440
is not just the amount
of energy they can store

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.860
but they also have to do it safely.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.170
Batteries also need to
be charged more quickly

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.390
and finally batteries
still aren't cheap enough.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:39.630
They probably need to be half the price

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.463
to be able to compete with
the gasoline powered engine.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.700
To accomplish all that,
a number of companies

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.600
are going inside the black box

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.460
and tinkering with those
four basic components,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.530
hoping to jumpstart the next
generation of batteries.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:56.960
Batteries have a long history
of pretty slow improvement

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.070
on the order of four to 5% a year.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.060
Think we're one of the few companies

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:01.893
that are actually trying

00:05:01.893 --> 00:05:04.240
to do something pretty
revolutionary in this space.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.230
Harold Rust company, Enovix,

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.270
based just South of San Francisco

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.440
is making one seemingly small tweak

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.540
to the lithium-ion battery.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:16.000
Replacing the anode typically
made of carbon with silicon.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.820
So the major advantage of silicon

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.170
is it has three times the
energy density of carbon

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.870
which it replaces so that
allows you to pack more stuff

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:25.810
in your battery and
drive up energy density.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.860
But silicon well, it's a great anode

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.190
suffers from a bunch of problems

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:34.450
and the biggest of which is
the fact that it expands 300%.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.760
Easy way to think about it is
when you're charging a cell

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.217
the silicon tends to expand
and when you discharge it,

00:05:41.217 --> 00:05:44.050
it compresses or contracts.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.950
That's a potentially
battery busting problem.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.900
But in Enovix claims to
have found a solution.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.790
A complex method of arranging
the batteries components

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:54.893
that keeps the silicon under pressure.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.880
This 3D architecture allows
us to constrain that expansion

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.290
in a very uniform way within the cell

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.940
that allows us to maintain
a very long cycle life.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.370
It allows us to basically
manage that swelling

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.580
without any macroscopic
growth of the battery.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.400
A silicon anode battery could
store about 50% more energy

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.130
than what's currently on the market.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.420
Which could mean we'll be
seeing lighter electronics

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.240
with longer battery
life in the near future.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.880
We've been actively sampling batteries

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:27.730
over the last two years to customers.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.770
We're sitting in a room
now where we're starting

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.280
to assemble our first production line.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.840
And right now we're
targeting first deliveries

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:36.500
towards the end of this year.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.320
But we're focused on consumer
electronics to start.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.750
With the technology it's
definitely applicable

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:43.730
to larger battery applications

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.350
like EVs potentially grid storage.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.800
And so that's on our roadmap.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:54.340
Elsewhere in Silicon
Valley, another company

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.253
is working on an even more
ambitious battery design.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.490
We started with the mission

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:01.970
of trying to narrow the gap that we

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:04.810
in combustion engine
based vehicles and EVs.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.810
And we recognized that the key there

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.110
was to build a better battery.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:11.140
We could usher in a new
era of transportation.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.200
15 minute charge times,
better life performance

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.480
and even lower costs.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.390
It turns out all those
problems can be addressed

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.860
if you just switch from a
carbon or carbon silicon anode

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.480
not to a lithium metal anode.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.070
Usually the lithium in
lithium-ion batteries only refers

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.580
to the molecule was shuttling
between the cathode and anode.

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:33.640
Making the anode itself out of lithium

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:36.800
could double the energy
density of the battery.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.180
A much bigger leap than
a silicon anode battery,

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:40.640
like in Enovix's.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.680
We didn't invent the idea
of a lithium metal battery.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.730
That idea has been around
for a very long time

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:48.113
even before lithium-ion
lithium metal batteries.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:50.713
There's just one small problem.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:53.800
Unfortunately, they're not safe.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:57.480
So typically inside a battery,
the electrolyte is liquid

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:01.370
but an liquid electrolyte
for a lithium metal battery

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:03.890
causes the lithium metal to degrade

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:06.662
and sometimes even short and catch fire.

00:08:06.662 --> 00:08:09.750
QuantumScape's main goal
was to try and replace

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.540
what is a liquid electrolyte
inside the battery

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.270
with a solid electrolyte.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.680
The problem is that no one
has been able to make one

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.870
that conducts well enough
to compete with the liquid.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.830
It wasn't clear that even the
material existed in nature

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:24.240
that could meet these requirements.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.610
So we had to explore a
wide range of materials,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:29.670
but luckily nature had a material

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:30.820
that meets the requirements

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:32.780
and our team was able to find it.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:36.300
It's literally a solid material,
it's a ceramic material,

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.300
but it's kind of a very special material

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:41.150
because lithium-ions can
just zip right through it

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:42.513
like they're on a highway.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.530
This single powered
cell is all QuantumScape

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:49.280
was able to show us of
they're solid state battery.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:51.690
Ultimately they'll stack
100 of these together

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.453
to make a complete battery pack.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.260
The company is still a few years

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.400
from selling a commercial product,

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.760
but the performance
improvements they're predicting

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.183
would be revolutionary.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.740
We've shown that we can charge faster.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.350
We can get 80% charge at 15 minutes,

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:09.640
which is gonna be really important

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:10.540
if you're on the road trip

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.537
or if you don't have a
garage to plug your car in

00:09:12.537 --> 00:09:14.030
and charge overnight.

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.430
You can get longer range

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.120
by improving the energy
density of the battery.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.620
What we've said is we're aiming

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:23.060
to have cars driving
with these cells in 2024.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:24.470
So the next few years will be

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.283
about increasing the scale of production.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:30.810
We formed a partnership
with Volkswagen in 2012

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.940
and they've announced that
they would partner with us

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.875
and make a joint venture
to commercialize the cells

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:38.113
and go into manufacturing together.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.970
We think that with these batteries,

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.290
you're gonna be able to get EVs

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.570
that compete more effectively

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.563
with combustion engine-based vehicles.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.480
You get more energy density,

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.770
you have lower costs and longer life.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:51.710
So our mission right now

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.920
is to get these batteries on the road,

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.610
try to really transform
the automotive sector

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:00.890
and in the process really
make a dent on CO2 emissions.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:06.210
Companies like QuantumScape and Enovix

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:09.480
are imagining a future in
which clean, affordable EVs

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.440
dominate the roads.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.700
But the implications go
much further than that.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.450
Better batteries are key
to almost every technology

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:19.730
that could slow down climate change.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:23.780
Batteries are what are known
as an enabling technology

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:28.330
which is that you can use
a battery to make an impact

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:31.210
across different sectors of the economy

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:34.180
and across different
types of technologies.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.200
So currently our batteries
can go in electric cars

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.980
but there's still a battery
that needs to go in a truck.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.620
Then there needs to be a
battery that goes in a ship

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:44.670
and then there needs to be a battery

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.003
that can go in a plane.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:49.280
There are now bigger and bigger batteries

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.050
being put on the grid
that help us increase

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.721
the amount of renewables

00:10:55.721 --> 00:10:59.520
And all those things get a boost

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.680
with every little innovation
that happens in batteries.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:06.293
And so the impact that
batteries can have is immense.

